{% from "components/icon.njk" import icon %}
{% from "components/nav-item.njk" import navItem %}
{#
  SITE HEADER — Figma 184:13318. Driven by _data/hero.js.

  Replaces the v2 floating bottom nav. That bar was a scroll-spy driven by
  scripts/nav.js; this is plain anchors with no JS at all. nav.js has since been
  deleted in the cleanup sweep, along with the site.nav array that fed it —
  that data is still in _data/site.js and is now unused.

  Hrefs are absolute (/#id) rather than bare fragments so the header behaves
  identically on /work/* and /projects, where those sections don't exist.

  TWO OF THE THREE POINT AT NOTHING RIGHT NOW. /#featured-works and /#contact
  were ids on the old Projects and Footer blocks, both removed in the sweep;
  they come back when Testimonials (187:2301) and Footer (289:2233) are built.
  /#about-me was repointed off the timeline's old #all-about-me at the same
  time and does resolve.

  The rule below the header and the rules either side of the container are
  three separate 1px borders in Figma at two different opacities — see
  _nav.css, where they are the only thing the two --fig-rule-* tokens exist for.

  data-theme IS THE LIGHT/DARK VARIANT of 446:37196, rewritten by
  scripts/header-theme.js as the header passes over each section. It ships as
  "light" rather than empty so the no-JS render is the correct one for the top
  of the page rather than a default that happens to be wrong.

  THE LOGO IS AN INLINE SVG, not an <img>, because it has to recolour with that
  variant and an <img> cannot inherit currentColor — see components/icon.njk.
#}

<header class="site-header" data-theme="light">
  <div class="site-header__inner">
    <a class="site-header__logo" href="/">
      {{ icon("logo-mark", size=hero.header.logo.size, class="site-header__mark") }}
      <span class="site-header__name"
        ><strong class="site-header__name-lead">{{ hero.header.name.lead }}</strong
        >{{ hero.header.name.rest }}</span
      >
    </a>

    {#
      THE MOBILE MENU BUTTON. Hidden above 480 and the only nav affordance below
      it. aria-controls points at the SAME <nav> the desktop bar uses — see the
      note on that element — so there is one set of links, not two.
    #}
    <button
      class="site-header__burger"
      type="button"
      aria-label="Menu"
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="site-menu"
      data-menu-toggle
    >{{ icon("menu-burger", size=24) }}</button>

    {#
      ONE NAV, TWO PRESENTATIONS. Below 480 this same element becomes the drop
      panel rather than a second copy of the links, and that is deliberate:
      scripts/header-theme.js binds the scroll-spy to
      `.site-header__link[data-spy]`, so a duplicate list would give every
      section two "current" items to mark and two elements to keep in sync.

      is-closed IS SCOPED, NOT GLOBAL. The class only hides anything inside the
      ≤480 block in _nav.css; above it the panel styling does not exist and the
      bar renders exactly as it always has, class or no class. A global .hidden
      would have hidden the desktop nav the moment the markup shipped closed.
    #}
    <nav class="site-header__nav is-closed" id="site-menu" aria-label="Primary" data-menu-panel>
      <ul class="site-header__links">
        {%- for item in hero.header.links %}
          <li>
            {#
              data-spy carries the bare id so header-theme.js does not have to
              parse the href — these are absolute (/#id) so the header works on
              /work/* and /projects, where the sections do not exist and nothing
              should ever be marked current.
            #}
            {# The label is navItem(), not a bare string, so the header and the
               footer roll from one definition — see components/nav-item.njk. #}
            <a
              class="site-header__link"
              href="{{ item.href }}"
              data-spy="{{ item.href | replace("/#", "") }}"
            >{{ navItem(item.label) }}</a>
          </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
